Subject: profile: an extensions axis, so a page's chrome.runtime is a persona and not the host's

A detector we met asserts that a "full" Chrome always exposes
`chrome.runtime` on an ordinary page, and that its absence is a headless or
automation tell. That is false, and it has been false since about Chrome 98.
Measured, both browsers, fresh `--user-data-dir`, `https://example.com/`:

    stock Google Chrome 153.0.8010.48   Apostate 152.0.7977.83
    getOwnPropertyNames(chrome)         getOwnPropertyNames(chrome)
      ["loadTimes","csi","app"]           ["loadTimes","csi","app"]
    typeof chrome.runtime "undefined"   typeof chrome.runtime "undefined"
    typeof window.browser "undefined"   typeof window.browser "undefined"

Identical on `file://`, and identical under `--fingerprint-platform=windows
--fingerprint=424242`. So this fork already matches fresh Chrome field for
field, and the rule fires on real Chrome users too. The folklore has a
source: the CryptoToken component extension shipped
`externally_connectable` `https://*/*` in every Chrome and Chromium build
until it was removed around Chrome 98, so for years every Chrome did expose
`chrome.runtime` on every https page.

What decides it today is one question asked of the installed extension set.
`CanWebpageContextConnectExternally`
(extensions/renderer/native_extension_bindings_system.cc) walks the
renderer's extensions and returns true on the first manifest whose
`externally_connectable.matches` covers the context URL;
`UpdateBindingsForContext` installs the `runtime` accessor and the `browser`
object from that answer alone. Measured on stock Chrome 153 with a
MetaMask-shaped manifest (`matches: ["http://*/*", "https://*/*"]`,
`ids: ["*"]`) loaded unpacked into a fresh profile:

    getOwnPropertyNames(chrome)  ["loadTimes","csi","app","runtime"]
    window.browser               object, own keys ["runtime"]
    window.browser.runtime === chrome.runtime   true
    chrome.runtime own keys      dynamicId, id, connect, sendMessage,
                                 ContextType, OnInstalledReason,
                                 OnRestartRequiredReason, PlatformArch,
                                 PlatformNaclArch, PlatformOs,
                                 RequestUpdateCheckStatus
    chrome.runtime.id            undefined  ('id' in runtime === true)
    file:// and about:blank      no runtime, no browser

field for field what `https://meet.google.com/` gives a fresh official Chrome
through the Hangouts component extension. MetaMask has tens of millions of
installs and the same shape appears in other wallet and automation
extensions, so both states are ordinary real-browser states: absent for a
browser with no matching extension, present for a wallet user.

So this is a persona and not a fix. It is drawn, absence weighted 75 and
presence 25, conditioned on nothing -- the same wallet extension is
installed on Windows, macOS and Linux desktops alike, so keying it on the
platform or the machine class would invent a correlation the population does
not have. The weights are authored and say so; no public dataset gives the
fraction.

Two things this deliberately is not.

It is not an extension list, and it never names an id. A fixed extension id
is a cross-instance correlator that survives every other axis: two launches
that share it are the same install whatever else was drawn. Shipping an
inert component extension to produce the observable would hand a detector
exactly that.

It is not an omission when it draws absent. The table serves
`externally_connectable: false` explicitly, because an absent section leaves
the host's own extension set to answer a question the persona is supposed to
own -- an operator running Apostate inside a profile directory that has a
wallet extension would otherwise present a persona that says nothing and a
page that says wallet.

WHAT THIS PATCH CONTAINS, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT

The profile half only: the schema key, the loader's parse and accessor, the
dispersion table, its catalogue registration and resolution-order slot, the
`--fingerprint-explain` line that falls out of being an axis, and the
reference resolver's mirror of the draw.

The renderer gate is NOT in this patch and NOT yet in the series, so no page
observes anything different for it yet: a composed profile carries
`extensions.externally_connectable`, `base::apostate::Profile` parses it,
and `Profile::extensions_externally_connectable()` answers -- with no caller.
The ledger row `extensions.webpage-runtime` records that state rather than
implying the surface is served. When the gate lands it is one predicate,
because everything downstream of it is already the real binding path:
`extensions/common/api/_api_features.json` already grants `runtime.connect`,
`runtime.sendMessage` and `runtime.lastError` to `web_page` over
`<all_urls>`; `runtime_hooks_delegate.cc` already installs `id` and
`dynamicId` as undefined-returning native data properties with an empty
setter; and `extensions/browser/api/messaging/message_service.cc` answers a
web-page channel to any id -- absent, present, or present but not
connectable -- with the identical "Could not establish connection. Receiving
end does not exist.", deliberately, so no enumeration oracle exists.

The axis draws from its own substream, keyed by its own label, so no
existing seed's other axes move: the compositor's `Draw(root, label, index)`
is per-axis by construction and `AddingAnAxisDoesNotShiftAnotherAxisChoice`
pins it. What does change for every seed is the composed profile's byte
digest, because it now carries one more section -- the native golden
digests in scripts/test_profile_resolver.py are stale until the next build
regenerates them, and the test reports that as a skip naming `extensions`
rather than passing quietly.

Verified: `scripts/checkfile.sh` on base/apostate/profile.cc and
base/apostate/compose_unittest.cc, `python3 scripts/check-schema-wiring.py`
at zero findings, and the reference resolver's new test over 60 seeds per
persona -- both states reachable, absence the majority, the same seed always
the same state, and the value a JSON boolean the loader will accept.
`base_unittests` was not run: this wave does not build, and the compositor
half of the evidence is the reference implementation, which does.

Ledger: extensions.webpage-runtime, automation.window-chrome-surface,
emit.extensions.native-bindings-webpage-runtime.

